  {
    "episode_id": 87422,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87422.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "It's critical to Wisconsin and Wisconsin workers. And let me share a number of reasons, some of which are Wisconsin specific, but others really apply to everywhere across this country. We have for years had a surface transportation policy that is not funded adequately to maintain the good condition of our roads and bridges. And that means that every year there's been deferred maintenance and it's piled on top of year after year. And now we have really a sub-par infrastructure in the United States. And I'm just now talking about surface transportation, roads, and bridges. We also know that we have other challenges and obligations. One of the things that I have observed in recent years as a U.S. Senator is extreme weather events, wiping out parts of our infrastructure, and we have programs to help the local governments quickly rebuild, but they're not able to use those funds to improve and make the infrastructure stronger and more resilient. So therefore, the next big storm, the next extreme flood, it's the same situation all over again. We're being pennywise and pound foolish. And so that's another challenge. We also have a situation with our drinking water and wastewater infrastructure where we've made a pledge to get rid of lead laterals for drinking water. And yet we have so far to go. And at the pace that Wisconsin, what does that matter? I think that we do have a long tradition of saying that when we use taxpayer dollars for our infrastructure, that we should",
