    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 87422,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87422.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'm cautiously optimistic myself, too. I will say that I think we're going to do it. I think we're going to do the bipartisan infrastructure framework. I hope even next week I am crossing my fingers that the bill will be ready by then and that we will initiate debates starting at the beginning of the week and push on through. And while we still don't know how strongly bipartisan the support will be, I'm feeling pretty encouraged by conversations I've been having that will have more than just the 10 Republicans we need to get across the finish line. So that will be, I think, an extremely confidence boosting set of actions. Again, I'm very hopeful. Now, beyond that, we are putting together a complementary measure through our budget process. And we hope to at least take the first steps to advancing that the following week. Wouldn't that be marvelous if we're able to do that? So what do I mean by that? Let me just say that this infrastructure plan is so bold that if we were to pass it but not invest in the workforce training and workforce of our nation, we wouldn't be able to get it done. If we want to remove all the remaining lead laterals in Wisconsin in a time horizon that really ramps and scales up compared to what we're doing now, we need more pipe fitters and similar plumbers and tradespeople to do that sort of work because we don't have today the numbers of people we need in order to get this job done. Same is true for laying fiber optics and getting broadband more extensively and building cell towers and all the things that we need to do to get broadband, let alone the roads and bridges of course that we've been talking about and the harbor maintenance, locks and dams. So we have to accompany this bipartisan infrastructure framework with a real commitment to workers and working families. I think it's going to be a moment of opportunity to also help people who have looked for an opportunity to retool their skillset and get a higher paying family supporting union job. It's going to be an opportunity for that to happen with some additional benefits to our environment, to our future economic strength. All right, I'm getting way ahead of myself, but I'm feeling optimistic not only about our first kick at the can, the bipartisan infrastructure framework, but going beyond that to make sure that we have the workforce that is able to rise to this challenge.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
